The ones I want it to handle are all on the CDROM. I don't necessarily
want it to do the update by default, but if the installation procedure
doesn't know if there is a new version that needs to be updated, what
chance do I have of figuring it out? At worst, an update would consist
of removing, then re-installing the whole product, or asking me which
subsets I want re-installed, after showing me what subsets I have
currently installed.

>|>setld doesn't even show me the actual titles for a set, just the titles for
>|>the subsets; but installupdate reports set names, not subset titles.
>
>Sorry, I don't follow you here.

Ok, here's the message I got when I started doing the update installation:
---
  The following layered products may require re-installation after
  the update installation has completed:

        DEC Ada Runtime Library
        ATOM Program Analysis Tools
        NetWorker SingleServer
        Development Enhancements for DEC OSF/1 V3.2 ( Rev 214 )
        DEC COBOL RTL V2.1 for DEC OSF/1 Systems
        DEC Pascal for DEC OSF/1 AXP Runtime Support
        DECladebug for OSF/1 AXP SYSTEMS, Version 3.0-16
        Multimedia Services for DEC OSF/1 V1.5
        X11R6 Advanced Development Kit

---
Now, I've got to go in and re-install each one of those separately,
possibly de-installing them first; but nothing in setld knows of things
with titles as given above. Example, DECladebug: it consists of three
subsets. In order to remove it, I have to do a setld -i and look for
things with DECladebug in the title, write them all down, then do a setld -d.
In the above case, I have to do that for quite a few subsets, all of them
with random sequences of letters and numbers.  There's obviously something
with an idea of what subsets are in the whole thing; something knows what
all subsets are in the "DECladebug for OSF/1 AXP SYSTEMS, Version 3.0-16"
set of subsets, since that's how the update installation identified it.

I guess what I'd like is:

a) be able to do "setld -l /cdrom/ALPHA" and have it figure out that there
are several sets of products available at that level of the directory, and
give me the choice of installing "DECladebug for OSF/1 AXP ... etc." without
me having to go in and issue 9 separate setld -l commands to hit each
directory. Even better would be to choose one and have it show the subsets
that could be loaded. Once I've specified ALL of the subsets in ALL of the
products, then it could go ahead and load them all.

b) something like "setld -d", which would give me an interactive menu-like
thing that shows me what SETS of things I have installed, and lets me specify
which ones to delete (similar to the way "setld -l" does when you only specify
the directory, and not the specific sets, except one level higher); again,
possibly two levels of menus so I can browse and choose which subsets in
each set I want to delete.



