Having just installed 4.0 on our development Alpha (2100 4/275), I went
ahead to see if I could compare the performance of the optimizer with
the ucode version. For some things, it improved a fair bit, but for most
the code it produced was a bit slower. I used -fast -O4 -inline speed
-tune host.  I didn't see a significant difference between -O3 and -O4.

I did have to hand-tune a bunch of "register" declarations for the old
compiler in order to get the maximum speed, and it was very sensitive
to any changes in that area, so I may be able to improve things still.

I also note that the assembler optimizations still don't really seem to do
much of anything other than rearranging order. For instance, it doesn't
seem to do any checking of redundant write-backs or loads even in the
most obvious and straightforward of cases (I was hoping the assembler
would be a bit smarter so I wouldn't have to put much effort along those
lines in generating the assembly code; the various .set options in the
assembler seem to imply it does a lot more optimizations than it seems
to actually do).

