|>a) why the heck didn't it just re-install them? I thought that was one of the
|>main points of an update installation! I could almost understand if it had
|>to get them off a different CD, but nope, they're all right there. Ok, so
|>ATOM is a little complicated, since you have to delete one subset and install
|>a different one, but I'd think that could be handled automatically pretty
|>easily (list obsolete subsets for a particular set).

The approach has been to not update layered products.  There are several 
reasons:  layered products don't always change on a release-to-release basis,
the installation procedure for them might include complicated steps which 
aren't easily supported by the update procedure, layered products aren't always
on the base OS CDROM, etc.

|>
|>b) is there no way for me to get a list of all the different sets, the way it
|>does for doing a full installation, show me which ones are already installed,
|>and then let me select them? I.e. almost like a full install, except only the
|>optional parts, show me what I've got loaded, and don't wipe out my system in
|>the meantime. Even if it can't show me what I've got loaded, at the very least
|>it could prompt at the SET level, spread over a bunch of sets, as the full
|>installation scripts do.

There is no way to do this.  An update installation simply updates the software
already installed on the system.  Of course, any new mandatory subsets are
installed as well.

|>
|>setld doesn't even show me the actual titles for a set, just the titles for
|>the subsets; but installupdate reports set names, not subset titles.

Sorry, I don't follow you here.

