Kostas Kritsilas (kritsilas@plntree.synapse.net) wrote:

: 	After finally getting the system operational, the need for a software
: load device for my VaxStation 2000 has now become clear. The unit has
: 6MB RAM, and one RD54 Hard drive ( I assume ~160MB). This system is
: running VMS 5.3.1 My particular unit also seems to have an extra
: section in the bottom of the case, which holds a small pcb, and a
: couple of cables which run out to the back of the section. One of
: these looks very much like a 3 row DB50 connector as used on older
: SUN3 Workstations, and the other looks like a Centronics type
: connector. I have the following questions:

: 1. Does this unit use SCSI for interfacing to external devices, and is
: there any way to detect the presence of a SCSI interface thorough the
: system software (maybe some form of the show device command)?
: Would the system have device drivers for standard SCSI tape
: drives/Hard disks?

The Centronics Connector goes to a TK50 tape unit.  It is similar
to SCSI, but is not true SCSI and the TK50 is the only device that
will work on it.  The DB-50 goes to an external RD5x drive and is
DEC proprietary (and most definitely not SCSI).

: 2. Barring the use of SCSI devices, what software load devices are
: usable on this system?

The only options are TK50 tape drive and over-the-network installation
from another VMS host.  You can also take the drive out and put it in
a larger MV II machine to copy software onto it.  I've done it all 3
ways.  If you have access to a MOP-MOM DLI Load Host, it's easiest to
boot the VS2000 over the network from a well-endowed server.

