Under Linux, select the Micro$oft sound system as a kernel option - here's
a sample /etc/soundconf for you:

/*      Generated by configure. Don't edit!!!!  */
/*      Making changes to this file is not as simple as it may look.    */

/*      If you change the CONFIG_ settings in local.h you       */
/*      _have_ to edit .defines too.    */

#undef  CONFIG_PAS
#undef  CONFIG_SB
#undef  CONFIG_ADLIB
#undef  CONFIG_GUS
#undef  CONFIG_MPU401
#undef  CONFIG_UART6850
#undef  CONFIG_PSS
#undef  CONFIG_GUS16
#undef  CONFIG_GUSMAX
#define CONFIG_MSS
#undef  CONFIG_SSCAPE
#undef  CONFIG_TRIX
#undef  CONFIG_MAD16
#undef  CONFIG_CS4232
#undef  CONFIG_MAUI
#undef  CONFIG_SPNP
#undef  CONFIG_UNUSED1
#undef  CONFIG_UNUSED2
#undef  CONFIG_AEDSP16
#define CONFIG_AUDIO
#undef  CONFIG_MIDI
#define CONFIG_YM3812

#undef  CONFIG_MPU_EMU
#define CONFIG_AD1848
#undef  CONFIG_SEQUENCER

#define MSS_BASE 0x530
#define MSS_IRQ 11
#define MSS_DMA 3
#define DSP_BUFFSIZE 65536
#define SELECTED_SOUND_OPTIONS  0x00880200


For other Linux/Alpha platforms, we do know that the kernel code
for Midi, sequencer, and most of the popular sound cards is correct.
The user-mode code for some midi players needed significant reworking
to get them working on a 64-bit machine, the last time I checked
(a couple of months ago)

	I hope this helps,
	
		Rick

