> 
> P.S.: We're already running a 8350 and are going to set up a MicroVax II and
>       a PDP/11, so we're not looking for big, antique machines (although they
>       have a certain flair, for sure... made my first net.steps on a 8200, but
>       that was long ago *sigh*), just some decent XTerminals...

A VAXstation II is just a MicroVAX II with a graphics board.  The VS II 
and VS 2000 both have about the same CPU performance. They are roughly 
equivalent to the 8200 you mention, or slightly slower than a single CPU 
in your 8350.

The VR262 is a monochrome graphics monitor which can be used with either 
the VS II/GPX or VS 2000.  The VS II/GPX can also use a color monitor 
(VR290 or VR299), as can the VS 2000 if it has the optional GPX board.

The VR241 is a color VT compatible terminal.  It can be used to access 
any of your VAXen, but it can not be used as an X-terminal.  I don't know 
what the PC500C3 or CM2086A3EX are.

A RRD50 is DECs original CDROM.  I don't know what it's interface is.  It 
probably isn't worth messing with.

The VS II and VS 2000 can both be used as X-terminals, but they are going 
to be fairly slow.  Also, the VS 2000 is limited in its memory options. 
Normally, you can only put 6MB in it, unless you can find one of the 12MB 
expansions for it.  With only 6MB, an X-server will tend to thrash pretty 
hard.

Also, the VS II and VS 2000 graphics are 1024x864.  You will need to 
decide if this is sufficient resolution for your needs.

If you can get a hold of a VS3100/SPX with a VRT19, you will be much 
better off.  This combination gives you 1280x1024 resolution and hardware 
accelleration.  (The GPX is also a hardware accellerator, but it doesn't 
work too well with the X-server.  The SPX is MUCH faster.)

Good luck!

