Brian 'Doc' O'Neill wrote:
> 
> I seem to remember this being asked before, but don't remember the source. I
> have three DEC 2000 model 300 units (a.k.a. AXP 150, Jensen) with 32Mb each,
> and while memory is cheap want to upgrade them to 64Mb, replacing all the
> 4Mb SIMMs with 8Mb SIMMs.
> 
> I believe they are standard 70ns 72-pin parity SIMMs - can anyone confirm?

We have an AXP150 with 32MB, configured as 4 8MB SIMMs. These are third 
party, 36 bit (i.e., parity), 70ns 72 pin SIMMs.  In other words, 
"standard" 72 pin parity SIMMs.

I have been told that I can use any 72 pin, 70ns, parity SIMMs, but I 
haven't tried to upgrade the memory yet, so I can't confirm this for 
certain.

I would make one suggestion:  If you want to go to 64MB, put in 4 16MB 
SIMMs.  Don't go the 8MB route.  This may allow you to keep 4 of the 4MB 
SIMMs, giving you 80MB of memory.

Generally, an 8MB SIMM is dual banked.  I don't know if the AXP150 allows 
you to put SIMMs in both sets of sockets when you use dual banked SIMMs. 
 If anybody knows the answer to this, I would appreciate hearing it.

