In article <4ps4i5$pqb@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>, shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca (Tim
Shoppa) wrote:

-> That's because, as it turns out, there is no such thing as Mode 4 EIO.
-> I just discovered that what the techie really meant was "Mode 4 PIO".
-> And the "PIO" does, indeed, stand for "programmed input/output",
-> i.e. the CPU is responsible for moving each and every byte from the
-> drive to memory - no DMA is involved.

Oh yeah... I remember reading some specs on it a while ago. It's really a
piece of crap. It's a great example of saving money on logic chips.

-> All the latest IDE hard drive ads, it seems, brag
-> about their "Mode 4 PIO" performance, and don't appear to contain a
-> single reference to "DMA".  "Mode 4 PIO" performance is also a big
-> selling point for Intel-based motherboards, it seems.

It's actually a cooperative between CPU and device, not just "Hey give me
this byte". It's "put this byte here". DMA was "Put this block of bytes
here". The reason Mode 4 PIO is a selling point for Intel motherboards is
that the Triton chipset offloads much of that from the CPU. It's still
slower than real DMA, but it seems that Pentium, DMA, and PCI don't mix.
