In article <4pst6e$njv@newsbf02.news.aol.com>, mhmccabe@aol.com (MH McCabe) writes:
> Don't let them kid you.  The VAX is a classic computer with a healthy
> following.  Somebody needs that system or parts.  I'd suggest posting a
> "for sale" message in alt.sys.pdp11.

  Only if you want to be inundated by twits, and I speak from experience.

  I've decommissioned various 11/750, 11/780, 11/785, and 8650 systems and
tried to give them away via the net. I've always specified that they were
available on a first-come, first-served basis and that the person(s) doing
the pickup needed a liftgate truck. Some of the more [less] amusing exper-
iences were:

  o Someone who came with a truck and no lift gate and thought they could
    lift the computer into the truck (a 780 is about 1000 pounds).
  o Someone who arrived in a Porsche, took careful measurements (and my
    computer room keys by mistake) and then took months to get me my keys
    back (and who decided he didn't really want the 785)
  o Someone who arrived by bus and expected to take the 8650 home the
    same way
  o Someone who rolled a 780 down the street and disassembled it in some-
    one's front yard, leaving junk all over the place

  This leaves out all of the people who called, asked if it could be used
"in a PC", etc.

  The only three successful cases I recall (and thanks to those people for
giving the systems a good home) were:

  o The University of Lowell, Mass gaming club, for a 780 and spare parts
  o Johnny Bilquist of Upsala University (Sweden!) for taking a pair of
    8650's (!!!)
  o Some guys from some school in PA who managed to take a 750, 2 TU77's,
    RA60's, etc. in a pickup truck

> As scrap, the cabinet alone is worth about $100 on the hacker market. 
> Depending on the condition, I'd pay about $500 for the CPU and console. 
> Disk Packs, tapes, and manuals are worth far more than the system itself.

  The person trying to dispose of the system needs to take into account the
whole package - even if you'd pay $500 for the parts you mentioned, how do
they get the rest of the system hauled away? Plus many of the people who
claim they want parts never show up, so the system is sitting around taking
up space.

  The best bet is to have someone take an inventory to see if there are any
valuable parts in the system, such as SCSI controllers, etc. and then see if
that person wants to bid on the complete system.

  Failing in that, see if your service provider wants it, or knows of some
other customer who'd like it for spare parts (or as a complete system).

  As much as I hate to see systems go for scrap when there are people who want 
to run them, there are so many clueless people out there that it takes for-
ever to try to find someone who can actually take the system.

