In article <4p7fe2$eo7@agate.berkeley.edu> marco@server.berkeley.edu (Marco E. Nicosia) writes:
> Hi there - I have a possible FAQ, but I don't have a solid pointer, so...
> 
> I have a machine that's currently running in UPGRADE security level.
> I'd *like* to be able to create a user with an initial password in
> /etc/password (usually an encrypted somethingorother that I've grabbed
> from another password file) and set their account to force them to
> change their password as soon as they log in at first, so that their
> password is shadowed out of /etc/passwd!
> 
> I know that this can be done, because when I upgraded the machine, it
> forced everyone to change their passwords in just such a manner. However,
> I've fiddled with the authmasks as much as I can, even read the security
> upgrade script, but I still have not discovered how to do this...
> 
> Does anyone have any advice? This is beginning to take more time than
> it's worth... Any good ideas will be much appreciated.

Just pop in the copied entry in /etc/passwd, and then do the following:

echo "${UID}:Nologin:1:${MINPASSLIFE}:${MAXPASSLIFE}:07:0:${UID}:00:00:00" | /usr/etc/sec/setauth

(With the variables set to appropriate values.) Then you can edit the entry in
the usual fashion.

/Bjorn
