If you're running OSF, then what you want is an mfs file
system. Here's a sample entry:

-s65536	/tmp mfs rw 1 0

This sets up a 32 MB filesystem for /tmp. It's taken out of
your swap space, so you'll need to be sure to have plenty.
It is swappable, but may perform better than regular disk
file systems. It sure seems to speed up our system.

Can't help you with VMS.

