In article <4promj$t5t@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>,
Glenn C. Everhart <everhart@star.enet.dec.com> wrote:
>
>I've noticed that Adaptec sends SCSI adapters out for PCs with DMA
>disabled by default; damfino if the drivers on msdos/windows/NT even
>allow DMA for them. If the virtues of DMA are well known in the PC
>world, I suspect they are nevertheless not used much.

As I understand it, DMA has been used in conjunction with the floppy
controller for years in IBM PC's and clones.  It's only recently, and
with special BIOS's and/or drivers, that DMA has caught on for I/O
to the hard disk.

(Please correct me if I'm wrong; last time I tried talking
to a PC-clone techie type, he was extolling the virtues of "Mode 4 EIO"
without being able to tell me if it was DMA or just a fancy name for
programmed input/output...)

>(Adaptec has something like 80% of the PC SCSI market share as I
>understand, BTW...this is not some fly-by-night tidbit...)

The name of the game in the PC world is to not break the delicate
special memory and I/O locations used by IBM in the original PC
and XT more than a decade ago.  Lots of software depends on being
able to poke a certain byte in a certain location to switch video
modes, say.  If somebody moved the location of that byte, all the
users would complain that the hardware was broken, not that the software
writers were idiots for doing I/O by peeking and poking bytes to hardware
locations rather than using the operating system/BIOS interface.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)




