> (Please correct me if I'm wrong; last time I tried talking
> to a PC-clone techie type, he was extolling the virtues of "Mode 4 EIO"
> without being able to tell me if it was DMA or just a fancy name for
> programmed input/output...)
> 

You mean Mode 4 PIO.  Just like you thought, it stands for Programmed I/O.
Typically, drives which are Mode 4 will also support a DMA mode (DMA Mode
2).  In typical PC world wisdom, almost no controller supports the DMA mode.

In any case, with DMA enabled, and an appropriate driver, the Adaptec 
controllers do DMA reasonably well.  Couple of gotchas, of course.  

- Ken



