In article <1996Jun10.155459@sisko>, wade@sisko.asub.arknet.edu writes:
|I have a VAX 4705 with VMS 5.5.  I have need of a server that I can hook
|4-8 modems to to allow PC's to call in and access the internet.  I was 
|told that there are terminal servers that support SLIP and PPP directly
|so the users can call them and go right to the net without having to
|go from the VAX.  This is what I wish to set up.  Does anyone have any
|recommendation as to what server to use?  I will be using generic modems.
|If DEC has an older product that I might find in the used market I would
|be intrested.  My budget has about had it and I need to get by as cheap
|as possible for about a year.
|
|Thanks for any suggestions!

This question would best be asked in the comm server 
newsgroup, comp.dcom.servers.

Most any modern terminal server should do the job.  You should
look for one that supports simultaneous modem control and
hardware flow control on each port.  Consider the top serial
port speed - you'll want at least 38.4Kbps, but speeds as 
fast as 115.2Kbps can be useful if using V.34 modems.

I believe that Digital DS300s, DS90s, and DS700s should be 
able to handle the job, with the right software.  Watch out
for some DS90s, though, as not all of them can do hardware
flow control on modem lines.  

Some other vendors of terminal servers that should meet your 
needs are cisco, Xylogics (Bay), Livingston, and Xyplex.

