Don Stokes wrote:

> Unix can run on non-split I/D machines, it just limits the total code,
> data & stack space of each process to 64kB, whereas with split I/D the code
> space is separate.  2.11bsd is kinda unhappy about the idea, but earlier
> unices certainly have worked.  And there's Venix for the Pro.

The problem is not only the user processes, but the Kernel.  None of the
implementations that support TCP/IP will work on a non-split machine
because
they run out of segments to do the overlaying.  You need one for the I/O
page,
one for u, a few for the non switchable parts, and one for the window
into
the mbufs. 

> And then there was Mini-Unix, which happily ran on MMU-less pdp11s --
> our Comp Sci dept had it (either 5th or 6th edn from Bell Labs) running
> on an 11/10.

MiniUNIX was not a real UNIX.  It was greatly thinned down to make it
work non-MM machines.  No pipes (were simulated in the shell)
and no preemption on the multitasking.  Yes, it worked, but it wasn't
even
as primative as real V6 was.



