Ed,

	You should really monitor your systems first to see where the 
bottleneck is. There's no sense in throwing money into a faster network 
card if the speed problem is being caused by excessive page swapping or 
something.

	Use	$ mon sys	for a while and try to determine where 
the problem is. Check the help within monitor for lots of good commands. 
You can redirect the output to a file and let it run for a couple of days 
and then view the file in quick time. Typically direct IO is disk IO and 
buffered IO is you network. For memory page faults, watch the vertical 
bar, if it moves away from the left edge of the scale on a regular basis 
then you propably need more memory.


Good luck 

Brian V


