In article <31BDE32C.289@lkg.dec.com>, Larry Stefani 
<stefani@lkg.dec.com> writes:

|>     We havd a 6 node mixed-interconnect/architecture/OpenVMS version
|>     cluster.  We'd like to speed up the access time to the NI drives in the
|>     cluster and are looking at adding FDDI cards to do this.  However,
|>     some of our nodes are VAX 4000-500s and since the ethernet controller
|>     is directly on the CPU board while the FDDI controller is on the Q-BUS
|>     we're wondering if the FDDI card would really give us faster MSCP
|>     response or not?

|The FDDI should do a little bit better considering that it's a 100Mb/s
|LAN technology over Ethernet's 10Mb/s.  However, it's my understanding
|that the max Qbus throughput is under 100Mb/s, so don't expect to see
|FDDI line speed on your system.

In fact, the Qbus is rated at a nominal maximum throughput of 3MB/sec
(24Mbps), so the very BEST you could get out of a Qbus FDDI
adapter would be just over twice an Ethernet.  (The SGEC Ether
chip on the 4500 *can* do a full 10Mbps.)  So I wouldn't expect
_thrilling_ improvements.

On the other hand, if all your VMS systems are sharing a single
10Mbps segment, then having them share a 100Mbps FDDI ring would
be an overall aggregate clusterwide throughput win, even if any 
given node could only do 20Mbps.

On the other other hand, a good cheap and easy way to boost your
aggregate clusterwide throughput would be to just drop in an
Ethernet switch.

Or maybe your access times are not slow due to network bandwidth
at all, but due to CPU or disk I/O/sec saturation.  In which case
going to FDDI or switched Ethernet would be no significant help.

You'd want to study your whole system - disk I/O request latency,
disk throughput, CPU saturation, network bandwidth utilization,
etc. - and figure out which part or parts of it need upgrading.

Aaron

