In article <4pjris$4f4@central.server.swt.edu>,
Ed Stuart <es12453@nyssa.swt.edu> wrote:
>    We havd a 6 node mixed-interconnect/architecture/OpenVMS version
>    cluster.  We'd like to speed up the access time to the NI drives in the
>    cluster and are looking at adding FDDI cards to do this.  However,
>    some of our nodes are VAX 4000-500s and since the ethernet controller
>    is directly on the CPU board while the FDDI controller is on the Q-BUS
>    we're wondering if the FDDI card would really give us faster MSCP
>    response or not?   

If 6 nodes are currently saturating the Ethernet, then going to FDDI
will certainly help.  Give the ETHERMON utility (available by anonymous
ftp from ftp.wku.edu) a try and see what it says about your
current network saturation.

If your ethernet isn't currently saturated, then the improvement you'll
see by going to FDDI will be small.  As you note, the Q-bus bandwidth
isn't all that large (~3Mbytes/sec).

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



