I have tried an IOMEGA JAZ on a VAXstation 4000/60 running VMS 6.1. MODISK
sees the disk, but MOUNT crashed under some circumstances (such as trying
to MOUNT/FOREIGN an uninitialized cartridge), and I don't believe I was
successful in initializing the cartridge.

However, I discovered that the DEC OSDS (Optical Storage Desktop Software)
product works just fine, even though DEC only claims it supports certain
MO disks. After I initialized the cartridge, I was able to mount and access
it with either OSDS or MODISK.

The OSDS license is not much more than the MODISK license ($500 vs. $395) and
it works with more VMS versions (i.e. 6.2).

By the way, I also tried the JAZ drive on an AlphaStation 200 4/100 running
VMS 6.2, and though VMS could see the drive, it would not initialize or mount
the cartridge.

There is another way you might get some of these drives to work. There is
a SCSI device type (such as disk, tape, removable disk, WORM disk) which
the device returns to the driver. I believe that VMS will not treat the
device as a disk unless the device type is disk (direct-access device). This
can be changed with an appropriate utility. I don't know if there is such
a utility running under VMS, but the SCU utility running under Digital Unix
(OSF/1) is an example, and I am sure there is a DOS equivalent.

