Tim Llewellyn wrote:
> Actually, it doesn't sound as if the original poster needs DFS, just
> integrated login.
> 
> We've been tinkering with DCE here, just test systems for now, but it
> seems one looses VMS specific security features like password history
> when using DCE authentication.

We made the migration on DEC UNIX C2 security to DCE Security.  We lost
password history and some other nice features.  But gained a coherent,
single database for passwords.  It wasn't easy and we suffered while
we sorted out some performance issues, bugy code from DEC and having
to make changes to some localy supported software.

I've also tinkered with bringing up DCE security on VMS.  Integrated
login works, with the caveat Tim mentions.  But we feel a little
burned by our experience on DEC UNIX, so we have decided to wait a
bit before tackling VMS.  VMS is used by administrative users and we
aren't eager to put them through any of the problems we encountered
on the UNIX side.

The other complaint I have is this, while VMS requires an entry in
both the UAF and the DCE registry to allow a DCE user to log in, DEC UNIX
requires an entry in the password override file to prevent VMS only
users from logging into the UNIX side.  This is something of an
administrative headache.

None of these problems will prevent us from moving in the direction of
implementing DCE, but we've slowed down a bit.

Bob Shafer



