John W. Manly wrote:

> I could live with that.  In fact, I could live with a heck of a lot if I
> could get true, integrated login on all my VMS, UNIX, and NT hosts.  One
> of the main things I'm wondering about, though, is how username length
> issues are handled -- do the VMS users have to be restricted to 8 characters,
> or does DCE somehow allow UNIX to work with login IDs longer than 8
> characters, or is there a mapping between the two, or what?

IDs longer than 8 characters are allowed, I'm not sure of the limitation, but
I have experimented with ids up to 14 characters.  There is a gottcha though.
There is a lot of software out there, some of it part of DEC's release, that
depends on the old /etc/passwd file still being there.  In fact, if you want
to keep the option of switching between DCE and BSD security then you are
stuck with the 8 character limitation.

Also, when we first brought up DCE security in a production environment, we
found our LAN was clobbered.  It turned out that every time any user did something
that needed to look at groups (e.g. ls -l) it was going to the security master to
grab the info.  We finally 1) made sure the /etc/groups file was consistent with
the registry and 2) modified the SIA matrix configuration to get the group information
using the BSD security library.

We also replicated the security master on all of our production hosts.  These steps
brought our network traffic down to tolerable levels.

Also, we could not get finger to work.  It would go into an infinite loop, apparently
trying to get the information from the security master.  We changed the SIA matrix to
use the BSD security library for the functions it needed as well.  In fact, our security
matrix is set up, basically to do login from DCE and most everything else from BSD.

So, even though we don't switch to BSD, except in an emergency and then only to repair
a problem, we are dependent on /etc/passwd and its 8 character limitation.

We have talked with DEC about this.  They say many of the problems are fixed in the
next release of DCE.  Which is in beta test.  However, it requires UNIX 4.0.  Which
will be out sometime during the summer (just which summer is not clear :-).

BTW - you will need the latest ECO from DEC for the current release of DCE to get even
this much working.

Bob Shafer



