John W. Manly wrote:
> I could live with that.  In fact, I could live with a heck of a lot if I
> could get true, integrated login on all my VMS, UNIX, and NT hosts.  One
> of the main things I'm wondering about, though, is how username length
> issues are handled -- do the VMS users have to be restricted to 8 characters,
> or does DCE somehow allow UNIX to work with login IDs longer than 8
> characters, or is there a mapping between the two, or what?
> 
> - JWM

I happened to think of one other gotcha that is a potential problem.

Integrated login on VMS does one nice thing for you that it doesn't
do on the DEC UNIX side, it automagically synchronizes the DCE password
with password in the UAF.  It does this at login time.  So, if you
do need to switch back to VMS only security, there is some chance
your users will be able to login with the same password.

The only problem is that by either using chpass on the VMS side, or
using passwd on DEC UNIX, it is possible to change your password to
something that VMS security doesn't like, thus the synchronization
will fail.  The user will still be able to login as long as you are
using DCE Security, but won't, at least with that password, when
using VMS only security.

chpass and passwd do not have much in the way of enforcing password
changing guidelines ala VMS or C2 security.  Thus special characters
that 'set password' normally rejects can be included in the password.
This causes the problem at synchronization time.

Bob Shafer



