newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <4p98sk$q04@pt9201.ped.pto.ford.com>, 
    		patteeuw@sys2.ped.pto.ford.com (Jack Patteeuw) writes:
> On page 5 of the AlphaServer 4100 Technical Summary  (a very interesting
> and well written document) there is a sentence that has me puzzled.
> 
>      "The 10-node system bus has slots for up to 4 CPUs and 4 memory
>       module pairs, and an I/O slot that connect to the I/O subsystem."
> 
> Last time I checked 4+4+1 was less than 10, unless your in base 9.
> 
> Anyone care to disclose what the 10th "slot" is used for ?

        I think the technical writer got that wrong.  In fact, a bunch
    of literature on the AS4100's is confusing on that aspect.

        In  the  version  of  the   glossy  "AlphaServer  4100  Systems,
    Technical  Summary" that I picked up at the May 6 (?)  announcement,
    on page 4, the opening paragraph reads:

       "Architecture
        The system bus has 10 nodes.  In the AlphaServer 4100
        systems four are used for CPU modules, four for memory
        options, and two for I/O."

    While the diagram on that page seems to show a single I/O connection
    to the bus, there are two I/O  busses, each of which handles a four-
    slot PCI bus (one of these has 1 PCI + 3 EISA/PCI slots, the PCI bus
    has  4  PCI-only slots).  The 3rd paragraph on that page makes  this
    explicit:

       "The I/O interface is implemented on one module, the system
        bus to PCI bus bridge module. Each I/O node is a separate
        PCI bus.  The AlphaServer 4100 systems have two separate
        64-bit PCI buses, one of which also offers an EISA bus."

    So the answer is, 4+4+2=10. :-)
                          ^

        BTW, having two separate I/O busses is a real plus for I/O.  You
    get 2 times  a  single  PCI  bus  throughput.   For 32-bit adapters,
    that's  something like 2x130 MB/s, but whenever 64-bit adapters  are
    available, you'll get 2x250 MB/s  (this  from  a  Digital  technical
    sales rep).  Not bad...

        If you haven't guessed yet, I'm  a  real fan of the AS4100.  Too
    bad we have to wait to get one.  :-(

        -Ken
