organization: cisco Systems (formerly TGV)
reply-to: Aaron@cisco.COM
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



In article <31B83DD0.41C6@ifk.tu-graz.ac.at>, Bernd Logar <logar@ifk.tu-graz.ac.at> writes:
|How can I start a rsh from my UNIX Alpha on a remote VAX VMS (different account) ?
|
|Thanks
|Bernd

Assuming that your Unix process is the rsh client and the
VMS system is the server, then

% rsh -l VMSUSERNAME VMSHOSTNAME VMSCOMMAND

Of course, the VMS system would have to be running a TCP/IP
package that supports rsh - such as our MultiNet product, for
example.  And the VMS system would have to be configured to
allow incoming access, however that might do that.  (With
MultiNet, that would involve having a 
unixhostname unixusername
rhosts entry in the target SYS$LOGIN.)

If the VMS system is the client, then you'd need to invoke
it as required by the VMS TCP/IP package.  If it's running
MultiNet, then you'd use

$ RSHELL /USERNAME=unixusername unixhost unixcommand

Again, the target Unix account would need to allow incoming
access from the client username/hostname tuple.

Aaron

