In article <31BC3F9B.6A8A@sensor.com>, Ron Natalie  <ron@sensor.com> wrote:
>Keith Pepin/DECwolf wrote:
>> H'm. Considering they use the same 11-on-a-chip that later MicroPDPs
>> did... The interrupt facility is unneeded on a Pro, since the bus is
>> simplified. Which also eliminates the need for DMA.

Huh?  You need interrupts to run any kind of reasonable OS, and while P/OS
is a dog, it's still fundamentally RSX.  I really can't imagine RSX
functioning without interrupts -- they're fairly fundamental to the whole
pdp11 "way of life".  I thought the Pro did have DMA, but I'm not at all
certain about that.

>We're talking about the T-11 here?

Pro 325 & 350 are F-11s (11/23), 380 is a J-11 (11/73), although the 380
lacks cache which hobbles the J-11 rather a lot.  Both processors are 
22-bit (so can address 4MB of memory -- I'm not sure what the Pro's 
memory busses can actually manage), but the F-11 lacks split I/D and
supervisor mode (it still has user & kernel mode).

>> -> UNICES required memory management (at least the PDP-11/40
>> -> style) and the later ones required a 45/70 style that
>> -> supported split I/D.
>
>I was referring to the PDP-11 implementations specifically.  Split-I/D
>(or as the PC-heads say, Medium Model) only are important on the
>machines with painfully small address spaces.

Unix can run on non-split I/D machines, it just limits the total code,
data & stack space of each process to 64kB, whereas with split I/D the code 
space is separate.  2.11bsd is kinda unhappy about the idea, but earlier 
unices certainly have worked.  And there's Venix for the Pro.

And then there was Mini-Unix, which happily ran on MMU-less pdp11s --
our Comp Sci dept had it (either 5th or 6th edn from Bell Labs) running
on an 11/10.

-- 
