In article <4pi994$uj7@totara.its.vuw.ac.nz>, don@rata.vuw.ac.nz (Don
Stokes) wrote:

-> In article <31BC3F9B.6A8A@sensor.com>, Ron Natalie  <ron@sensor.com> wrote:
-> >Keith Pepin/DECwolf wrote:
-> >> H'm. Considering they use the same 11-on-a-chip that later MicroPDPs
-> >> did... The interrupt facility is unneeded on a Pro, since the bus is
-> >> simplified. Which also eliminates the need for DMA.
-> 
-> Huh?  You need interrupts to run any kind of reasonable OS, and while P/OS
-> is a dog, it's still fundamentally RSX.  I really can't imagine RSX
-> functioning without interrupts -- they're fairly fundamental to the whole
-> pdp11 "way of life".  I thought the Pro did have DMA, but I'm not at all
-> certain about that.

I wasn't saying that the Pro didn't have either DMA or interrupts. When I
made a comment about UNIX on a Pro, someone said that interrupts weren't
supported and neither was DMA. Well, the Pro DOES have interrupts, though
on a limited bus like that you CAN get away without it. As a project, a
friend and I designed a simple computer, with no interrupts. It was based
on a 6120. Fun to play with, and had a neat single step feature. Rob had
the idea of using a few 74xx chips to make a counter, and we rigged it to
bit 4 of 7:7777 [a lot of stuff was in 7:7777, actually, we used
7:7770-7:7777 for various things]. Tickling bit 4 of 7:7777 caused the
thing to delay for enough for the thing to load the original PC and AC,
then it would give a clock tick for the instruction. Trying to support
multiple-cycle instructions, though, turned into a real pain. I forget how
in the hell we solved that problem... DMA isn't all that necessary... my
statement of the stuff being unneeded on a Pro should have said "unneeded
by the OS", because the OS really DOESN'T need interrupts, just SOME
mechanism of I/O.

-> >We're talking about the T-11 here?
-> 
-> Pro 325 & 350 are F-11s (11/23), 380 is a J-11 (11/73), although the 380
-> lacks cache which hobbles the J-11 rather a lot.  Both processors are 
-> 22-bit (so can address 4MB of memory -- I'm not sure what the Pro's 
-> memory busses can actually manage), but the F-11 lacks split I/D and
-> supervisor mode (it still has user & kernel mode).

I know the 350 memory bus goes to 1MB. The 380, I have NO idea. More
accurately, the 350 _cards_ go to 1MB. Maybe by fudging with the chips you
can get more.

-> Unix can run on non-split I/D machines, it just limits the total code,
-> data & stack space of each process to 64kB, whereas with split I/D the code 
-> space is separate.  2.11bsd is kinda unhappy about the idea, but earlier 
-> unices certainly have worked.  And there's Venix for the Pro.

Does Venix support the I/D distinction of the 380? What other UNIXes are
Pro-friendly? I was hoping for actual DEC ULTRIX-11, I tend to like DEC
stuff. I'm a great fan of OS/8. I'm picking up a DECmate II just for the
fun of OS278. Which still uses CCL.

-> And then there was Mini-Unix, which happily ran on MMU-less pdp11s --
-> our Comp Sci dept had it (either 5th or 6th edn from Bell Labs) running
-> on an 11/10.

Heh heh. I am mainly interested in putting a Pro on a network with my VAX
and having fun with it. Though I would just as gladly take P/OS and
DECnet.
