In article <4ov588$gqr@web.cae.ca>, Bruno Gallichand <brunog@cae.ca> writes:
> Hi,
> 
>   I recently bought an old Decmate III but it came without any disks or
> software.  I was told to look in ftp.update.uu.se to find software for
> it and the most important software was in fact there.

Yup! 
 
>   My problem is that I really have no clue of how to setup or format
> diskettes for it.  I've found a device driver and program to read/write
> decmate diskettes in a PC's 5 1/4 in drive but it cannot 'format' the
> disks for usage on a Decmate as the PC's controller doesn't allow it.
 
>   Does anyone know how to format Decmate III diskettes?

Yup!   Lots of people do.

There are several possibilities...
1   get a Rainbow, they of all the RX50 equiped machines that DEC sold
are actually capable of formatting from scratch a floppy.
2  get a program to format RX50 floppies on you PC, assuming you have
one.  Can't remember the name, 'cos I have a Rainbow. ;-)
3  use Teledisk running on a PC.... see below.
 
>   Also, I'm not familiar with the operation of this small computer, e.g.
> how files are organized, executable program synthax (... what is a TD0 file
> for example, what is wps,...).  If anyone can help me find documentation 
> about such topics I would appreciate.

A .TD0 file is the output of the PC program Teledisk. It is a file which is
an image of a floppy. The idea is that you have a PC with a decent 5.25"
floppy drive, and Teledisk. You feed in, say, and RX50 (DEC) type floppy
and Teledisk makes it into a file....obviously the reverse process takes
the file and writes a floppy....in this process, a suitable floppy gets
all the initialisation it needs.
 
WPS is a Word Processing System....most DECmates were sold mainly as WPS
type systems.   You can also run a version of the OS-8, called OS-278,
or COS-310.  Given a co-processor board (which is reasonably common)
you might also be able to run CP/M.

As for doco....I have a fairly complete set spare in my garage, but, from 
Sydney Australia, it would cost a fortune to mail.  :-(

