Mike Barton (acc_mwb@exodus.valpo.edu) wrote:


: I'm going to be working for a company soon that has a VAX machine that I will
: use to do embedded systems software development.  I'm experienced in the
: software development, but I'm a little unsure about the tools I'll have to use. 
: I'm pretty familiar with the command line VAX/VMS interface from a remote
: terimal, but I've never used a VAX machine as a workstation.  In fact, it
: didn't even have OpenVMS installed on it.  I've heard of DecWindows before and
: OpenVMS and I've read what I can of the FAQ for the comp.os.vms and
: comp.sys.dec newsgroups.  I've had experience with Ultrix and HP-UX command
: line and gui environments before.

When you say it didn't have OpenVMS, do you mean it had ULTRIX?  c.f.  the FAQ,
VMS and OpenVMS are the same thing.

If you've used DECwindows on ULTRIX (the gui), DECwindows on VMS is about the 
same.  Biggest difference I know of is that VMS's FileView is much more 
powerfull and easy to customize than the ULTRIX version.  DECwindows and
HP-UX gui are both X11 windows.  Biggest difference there is that HP doesn't
seperate window manager from session manager, which limits the options 
available to X terminal users.

: The other interesting twist on this matter is the fact that normally this
: company uses PCs.  This VAX/VMS environment along with the complier/assembler
: and development environment for this project was specified by the customer. 
: The computer services department doesn't support this machine, because it's the
: only one of its kind at this company.  

Smart customer, IMHO.

: I know that they're using a VMS graphical interface, so I assumed OpenVMS.  Is
: that a good assumption here?  I think the machine is a VAX 4000.  It's got a
: tape backup drive, internal hard disk, and an added on hard disk.  

I don't think 4000 run VMS old enough to support VWS, the predecessor to 
DECwindows.  So I'd bet it's DECwindows.

: I'll be responsible for the maintenance, backups, etc. of this machine.  All
: the manuals are there.

: Any suggestions on where I can learn more about the OpenVMS environment?  I
: suppose I'll be able to run a command line and work through some of the stuff
: where I'm somewhat familiar with what's going on.  Another thing that'd be good
: to know when I sit down in front of the machine is how to get a directory
: structure of the hard drives, find out what disks are available, and what their
: names are.
: Any help where to look, what to read, or what I should do when I sit down at
: the machine would be greatly appreciated. 

Read the introductor manuals from each of the User's set, System manager's set,
and Programmer's set.  Then read the introductory section from the manuals 
which seem to apply.  The most important think to learn is which manual to look
at when you need to know something new.


