bUrD uPh daAr n0rtH (falc@attila.tsunami.org) wrote:
: I splurged at a surplus auction -- I spent $5.00 and got myself a 
: VR-290-DA, a VR-299, and two VR-297-DAs.  I've previously converted a
: VR-290-DA for use as a fixed-frequency monitor under linux (at 1024x864);
: I'd like to do the same for the other monitors.  The VR-299 looks like it
: takes the same monitor timings as the VR-290-DA; is this indeed the case,
: or is there some subtle difference that will make it explode?

( ... stuff deleted ... )

 Ok, here they go:

   VR297:

   Horizontal rate timing:

        Frequency    		:   54.04 Khz
        Front porch  		:   160 ns
        Sync pulse   		:   1,850 us
        Back porch   		:   1,680 us
        Blanking interval	:   3.70 us
        Active video 		:   14.8 us

   Vertical rate timing 

	Frequency    		:   60 Khz
        Front porch  		:   0 lines
        Sync pulse   		:   3 lines
        Blanking interval  	:   37 lines
        Active video 		:   16 ms
        Vertical period		:   16.67 ms
   
   Sync on green
   Red/blue: 0.714 Vp at 75 ohm
   Green: 1.0 Vp at 75 ohm 
   Resolution : 1024x684


   VR299:

   Horizontal rate timing:

        Frequency    		:   54.04 Khz
        Front porch  		:   159 ns
        Sync pulse   		:   1,850 us
        Back porch   		:   1,680 us
        Blanking interval	:   3.69 us maximum
        Active video 		:   14.8 us
        Horizontal period	:   18.5 us

   Vertical rate timing 

	Frequency    		:   60 Khz
        Front porch  		:   0 horizontal lines
        Sync pulse   		:   3 lines
        Blanking interval  	:   37 lines
        Active video 		:   16 ms
        Vertical period		:   16.67 ms

   Sync on green
   Red/Blue/Green : 0.7 Vp 75 ohm
   Resolution : 1024x684

   Sorry, no info about the other one.
   These specs came from the DECStation 5000/200 Hardware manual.
   
   NO GARANTIES ( But you already knew that , didn't you ? )
   
   I have 6 VR299 and I don't like them. Too much glare, low refresh.
   The VRT19 or the VR320, now these are good monitors...
   BTW, did you use that circuit that sometimes is posted here 
   ( the schematic is in the fixed frequency video FAQ ) to connect 
   the monitor to the card?
   Or did you use some special card ( Mirage, Photon ) ?
   If you did use the circuit give me a call. I will probably try one off
   my VR320 with it one of these days, and I liked to known if someone 
   already tried and it worked ( or at least didn't blew anything...)
   And did you really paid just 5 bucks ???
   That kind of things never happens to me...
   Hope this helps and happy viewing,

