On Dec Unix 3.2C, we have the following problem: we can't get all the
stackspace that we are supposed to get.

To be precise: the following program will coredump with a segmentation
violation

        program cat
        real x(12*1024*1024/4)
        x(1) = 1.
        write(6,*)x(1)
        stop
        end

This program, if compiled with f77 -automatic will die when running.  If
I omit '-automatic' (which then causes x to be allocated in bss), then
it runs fine.  If I reduce the size of the array to 11 megabytes, it
runs fine.

We have lots of RAM, so that's not our problem.  Our system has been
configured with sysconfig to have a maximum stackspace of 32 meg.  Here
is what limits reports:
% limit  
cputime         unlimited
filesize        unlimited
datasize        131072 kbytes
stacksize       32768 kbytes
coredumpsize    unlimited
memoryuse       250088 kbytes
descriptors     4096 files
addressspace    1048576 kbytes

% limit -h
cputime         unlimited
filesize        unlimited
datasize        1048576 kbytes
stacksize       32768 kbytes
coredumpsize    unlimited
memoryuse       250088 kbytes
descriptors     4096 files
addressspace    1048576 kbytes


Does anyone have an explanation/fix for this?  What else needs to be
tuned?
